Four guests from England, France, Japan, and Germany, named A, B, C, and D, happened to meet. Besides their native languages, each of them can speak one of the other three countries' languages. There is one language that three of them can speak, but there is no language that all of them understand. It is known that:
1. A is Japanese, D cannot speak Japanese, but they can communicate freely;
2. Among the four, there is no one who can communicate in both Japanese and French;
3. When A, B, C, and D communicate, they cannot find a common language;
4. B cannot speak English. When A communicates with C, B can translate for them. Based on the languages they understand, the correct reasoning is (　　)
A: A speaks Japanese and German, B speaks French and German, C speaks English and French, D speaks English and German
B: A speaks Japanese and English, B speaks Japanese and German, C speaks German and French, D speaks Japanese and English
C: A speaks Japanese and German, B speaks French and German, C speaks English and German, D speaks English and German
D: A speaks Japanese and French, B speaks English and German, C speaks French and German, D speaks French and English

